WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:10.950 align:start position:0%
 
thank you  georges i'd like to thank the presidency  also for the excellent cooperation we've  been enjoying over the past few months  together  and good morning everybody  now for two years now  i've enjoyed the trust of president  juncker president trusk and the members  of the european council as well as the  european parliament in my position  i have attempted to operate methodically  and calmly  to ensure that the decisions of the  european union as expressed  by the european council in its decisions  and guidelines on the one hand  and also by the european  parliament in a number of its  resolutions i've attempted to  pursue these in an orderly fashion now  if we look at these guidelines and  decisions  they reflect first of all the desire to  be united  and that has been expressed since  june 2016 by the european institutions  and in my capacity i am i  am a worker hopefully a qualified worker  on behalf of the 27 in nurturing this  unity on a daily basis  and ensuring transparency interest  as part of the process of dialogue  so we're talking about unity we're  talking about defending the interests of  the european union  and indeed the foundations of the  european union which the uk  is very familiar with because we've been  constructing these for the past 44 years  we're talking about the single market in  all of its dimensions which is our main  achievement  in the current global context and for  the future  an orderly withdrawal of the united  kingdom  this is something which has on a daily  basis  been our objective and it remains  our objective obviously  an orderly withdrawal implies  the best possible conditions for the  second set of negotiations equally  important in my mind  the negotiations for the future  partnership with the united kingdom  which will would and will remain a  friend partner and ally  so this explains  how and why we have been working on this  for the past two years now  on the eve of the european council  we have examined the situation in london  and president trusk has given  theresa may the opportunity to to to  show the way forward  timeline a road map this is something  the european union needs  in order to enable it to take the right  decisions  now a new element in this very very  serious and complex  context we're operating within  is of course the fact that we have  had cross-party discussions  initiated finally with labor  by theresa may  we all expressed our hopes and  expectations  in this regard  in other words with regard to these  cross-party negotiations  our expectations and our hope  that this dialogue will conclude with a  positive result  which will allow us at last to have  a positive majority emerge  with regard to this withdrawal agreement  and this is something i was able to  reiterate this morning  to ministers if the uk wishes to leave  the eu in an orderly fashion the only  way to do so is the base  is based on the withdrawal agreement  which we agreed upon together  so as i said this cross-party dialogue  is the new element which  could lead to a request which we would  be then willing  not only to agree to but also to  implement for our future elite relations  you will have heard me say on a number  of occasions first of all  that the withdrawal agreement is not  going to be reopened  is not up for negotiation again that  continues to be the case secondly the  political  declaration which will set the framework  for future relations  can be improved we can  provide it with increased an increased  level of ambition  if that is the wish of the uk  clearly there is one point which i think  needs to be stated quite clearly and  that is  that these ambitions with regard to  future relations  which could for example consist of  adding and i say here  adding to  a free trade agreement on which we've  agreed with  the uk already that's a minimum level in  other words it would avoid  involve adding to this free trade  agreement  for example a customs union a genuine  customs union  and we are willing to  improve and amend the political  liberation rapidly within a few hours  or days  but the request we await and expect  from the uk as a result of this  cross-party dialogue is one which needs  to respect  the principles underlying the approach  of the european council  and the european parliament and respect  what we are and what we will continue to  be  and i think this is something worth  recalling  we're talking here about the integrity  of the internal market  the decision-making autonomy of the 27  and the indivisibility of the four  freedoms  we are not willing to compromise  in any way the very foundations of the  european union  which are based on this single market  and the the genuine ecosystem which  it entails which protects businesses and  in particular  protects consumers that's a point which  needs to be  understood very very clearly and if  that's the case then we  are willing straight away and this is  something president juncker has said on  a number of occasions he said this  recently at the plenary of the european  parliament we  are ready straight away to get down to  amending and improving and providing  more ambition to  the political declaration which would  set the framework for future relations  now why am i stressing this particular  point and  and these new elements in the process  which we hope will allow for a new  process to emerge in the house of  commons  and will allow ultimately for  a new majority to emerge with regard to  this agreement and this new political  declaration  why am i stressing this so much well  clearly it's because this is what would  provide meaning to the request which the  uk prime minister has submitted for an  extension  and this is all i wish to say on this  for today  forgive me for not dwelling on this  further but this is a matter  for the leaders of the european union  tomorrow  this extension has to serve a purpose  it's got to serve a purpose to provide  more time if necessary to ensure that  the political process  i've described can be crowned with  success  and that this majority can be attained  the issue of the length of the extension  which has been discussed at great length  with its possible pros and cons  i'm not going to make a statement on  that  from from my vantage point it's not up  to me to do so but  the duration of the extension has got to  be in line  in any case with the purpose being  striven for that's what i would say  right now  it's got to to to serve a purpose or a  name  no a final word one of the ministers  said something this morning  the aim of all this is to  achieve an orderly withdrawal  and i i would prefer to say this rather  than to  imply that our objective is to avoid a  no deal because i know there will never  be  a decision taken by the european union  and no deal  would never be a decision taken by the  european union we have been working  towards  an agreement a treaty if you like  not just a deal we're talking about 600  pages or so  setting out very specifically guarantees  for citizens for ireland  for the beneficiaries of european funds  and a lot of other domains  which could be jeopardized so it's more  than a deal it's a it's a genuine treaty  we have worked towards this treaty  together with the british  and that means that no deal would never  be a decision  of the european union it would  always be the responsibility of the  united kingdom to tell us what they want  if they want to stop the brexit process  they can do so they can revoke  article 50 if they want to  allow for a deal to take place or indeed  to prevent a no deal  as we've heard from the house of commons  if they want to avoid a no deal  situation  then they've got to vote in favor of the  agreement  with as i said the improvements to the  political declaration that's their  responsibility and it will remain the  responsibility of the united kingdom  and of the uk only so that's what i  would say at this point  in this very um constructive  debate in a spirit of solidarity  i was very very touched by the  expressions of trust and confidence  by all the ministers for the work of our  team which of course is their team  and has been for the past two years

